To set up dark mode in your UI, you'll need to modify both CSS backgrounds and colors as well as `text-color` to ensure proper contrast. Here’s a simple example:

```css
body {
    background-color: #000; /* black background */
}

body {
    color: #fff; /* white text */
}
```

For a dark mode toggle, you'll need to detect whether the user has dark mode enabled on their system and apply different styles accordingly. You might want to use a tool like prefers-color-scheme, which is supported in modern browsers.

```css
@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
    body {
        background-color: #000; /* black background if dark mode enabled */
        color: #fff; /* white text if dark mode enabled */
    }
}
```

In this example, if the user has dark mode enabled, the styles within the media query will be applied, otherwise the default styles will be used. Keep in mind that user preferences should take precedence over your application's settings.

```css
@media (prefers-color-scheme: light) {
    body {
        background-color: #f7f7f7; /* default background color */
        color: #333; /* default text color */
    }
}
```

If your application has a light mode too, you'll need to add a separate media query for light mode as shown above. If not, you may omit it, although consider setting default styles in case mobile devices or older browsers don't support `prefers-color-scheme`.

Please note that you should include a color scheme media query for officially supporting the WCAG AA accessibility guidelines.

```css
@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
    body {
        background-color: #000;
        color: #fff;
    }
}
```

You should also specify a light scheme fallback where dark is not supported `@media` query.

```css
@media (prefers-color-scheme: light) {
    body {
        background-color: #f7f7f7;
        color: #333;
    }
}
```

Also note `body { background-color: #f7f7f7; color: #333; }` as default light color scheme

